1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,215
Filters speak Servlet. Interceptors speak
Spring MVC: handlers, handler mappings,
and the lifecycle around controller
invocation.

2
00:00:09,335 --> 00:00:20,327
The port operations console makes that
distinction concrete — you want timing on
/admin/ handler methods, not on every
static asset and health ping. Recall the
dispatcher pipeline.

3
00:00:20,448 --> 00:00:32,472
After HandlerMapping resolves a
HandlerExecutionChain, that chain includes
interceptors. DispatcherServlet calls
preHandle on each interceptor before the
HandlerAdapter invokes the controller.

4
00:00:32,591 --> 00:00:45,960
After a successful invocation it calls
postHandle. After completion — including
when an exception was thrown — it calls
afterCompletion. That timeline is inside
MVC, after a handler was chosen.

5
00:00:46,079 --> 00:01:01,128
Look at this java for a moment. Look at
this java for a moment. Path patterns on
the registry are the point of this
lesson’s scenario: only /admin/ pays the
timing tax. Berth boards and public parcel
GETs stay quiet.

6
00:01:01,247 --> 00:01:14,015
Returning false from preHandle aborts the
chain — useful for annotation-driven
gates, dangerous if you forget to write a
response. A second interceptor pattern
clarifies why filters cannot replace this
layer.

7
00:01:14,135 --> 00:01:24,551
Suppose only methods marked @Audited
should emit console audit events. In
preHandle, cast handler to HandlerMethod,
look for the annotation, and stash a flag.

8
00:01:24,671 --> 00:01:34,128
In afterCompletion, if the flag is set and
ex is null, write the audit record. A
servlet filter never receives that handler
method metadata.

9
00:01:34,247 --> 00:01:45,071
postHandle runs only after a successful
handler return and before the view is
rendered — less useful for pure
@RestController JSON, still useful for
adding model attributes on view
controllers.

10
00:01:45,191 --> 00:01:56,903
Prefer afterCompletion for timing and
cleanup because it runs on success and
failure. Registering the same logic as
both a filter and an interceptor "just in
case" doubles work and muddies logs.

11
00:01:57,023 --> 00:02:07,007
Putting security authentication solely in
an interceptor also fails closed paths
that never map — authentication that must
run even when no handler exists remains
filter work.

12
00:02:07,127 --> 00:02:19,127
And measuring every path under / recreates
the noise you avoided by scoping to
/admin/. Servlet filters cover early HTTP
wrapping; MVC interceptors cover handler-
aware hooks.

13
00:02:19,247 --> 00:02:29,111
One request shape still sits outside
ordinary JSON bodies: clients upload files
— bill-of-lading PDFs — as multipart
streams with size limits and different
binding rules.

14
00:02:29,231 --> 00:02:33,383
How Spring exposes that without hand-
parsing MIME is next.
